    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ccd326c4_2fb84690",
        "filename": "cc/object_sdk_member.go",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 25,
      "author": {
        "id": 1373864
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-03-16T21:40:00Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027ve looked a bit more into it, but I\u0027m not sure it\u0027s a good idea. The cc_library code contains lots of logic to deal with shared vs static, headers, sdk_version, stl, and probably more. None of that is applicable to prebuilt objects (at least not for my current examples in bionic/libc/Android.bp). I suspect the extra flags and checks to support these different code paths are worse than the duplication here, and it\u0027s likely to get more involved over time.\n\nAlso, there\u0027s duplication with IsInstance and maybe a few other functions, but I think that\u0027s an issue for all the modules in cc/ - they should extend a common ccSdkMemberType to deal with that, but that\u0027d be a separate cleanup.",
      "parentUuid": "e87a3786_6c4879ea",
      "revId": "2598b7091beb47a50a184037d3a31b412d8ddc16",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "5b39a871_f2b76ded",
        "filename": "cc/object_sdk_member.go",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 25,
      "author": {
        "id": 1039338
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-03-17T13:38:40Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The cc_object, cc_library, cc_library_shared, cc_library_static, cc_library_headers are all supported using the same code base with some tweaks to cope with the different criteria of each.\n\nCurrently, the sdk member support for cc_library (when submitted), cc_library_shared, cc_library_static, cc_library_headers all use the same code base in a similar way.\n\nIt would be very inconsistent for cc_object to be different to all the others. It opens up the possibility that differences in behavior will creep in between cc_object and the others, makes any refactorings of the sdk (more are coming) slightly more expensive and can lead to further duplication if it does need to support functionality that is already supported by librarySdkMemberType.\n\nThe librarySdkMemberType code can already handle cc_library_headers which does not have the concept of static/shared so that is already in there. It would need tweaking to differentiate between cc_library_headers (no link types, no object file) and cc_object (no link types, has object file).\n\nYou also mentioned \u0027stl\u0027 but based on your test you specify stl: \"none\" on the cc_object but it does not seem to be supported on cc_prebuilt_object. If it has no effect on cc_object then it should not be specified on it for the test. If it does have an effect on cc_object then maybe it should also be supported on the cc_prebuilt_object.\n\nI understand where you\u0027re coming from with the library code becoming overly complicated trying to deal with too many different types (and the binary/library duplication of some code) but I think a better solution is to refactor the library code to extract what is common and push the type specific functionality down to each type not simply duplicating all of this code.\n\nWhat are the specific tweaks needed in librarySdkMemberType to support cc_object?\n* Differentiate between cc_library_headers and cc_object.\n\nAnything else?",
      "parentUuid": "ccd326c4_2fb84690",
      "revId": "2598b7091beb47a50a184037d3a31b412d8ddc16",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4e73c674_2d40eab3",
        "filename": "cc/object_sdk_member.go",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 25,
      "author": {
        "id": 1373864
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-03-19T04:10:37Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It\u0027s a matter of trade-off. I gave it a go, and this far it\u0027s reasonably simple but still had some illustrative snags I\u0027ll discuss below. It\u0027ll get worse though as the code gets more complicated, unless we\u0027re careful.\n\n\u003e It would be very inconsistent for cc_object to be different to all the others.\n\nSide point, but doesn\u0027t that apply to cc_binary as well? In several ways cc_binary is closer to cc_library than cc_object is.\n\n\u003e can already handle cc_library_headers which does not have the concept of static/shared so that is already in there.\n\nI had to add a new flag anyway, because the existing linkTypes handling coupled the lack of that with the lack of output files, which doesn\u0027t work for cc_object.\n\n\u003e If [stl] has no effect on cc_object then it should not be specified on it for the test.\n\nThe real cc_object\u0027s I need to support set stl:none, and as you note cc_prebuilt_object doesn\u0027t support it. Hence the snapshot builder needs to filter it out, and then the tests should test that.\n\nIn PS6 I just added an ugly bool to skip stl propagation. It\u0027s only for illustrative purposes - I don\u0027t intend to submit that. I can see a couple of options here:\n\n1. Add stl to cc_prebuilt_object, even though it\u0027d do absolutely nothing there. Ugly design-wise and confusing for people reading the bp files, but keeps things simple. This already seems to be a pattern, e.g. cc_library_headers has shared_libs, static_libs, and many other properties that are meaningless but possible to set and - worse - documented on go/Android.bp.\n\n2. Implement more or less generic property filtering. The fancy way would be to use reflection to detect which properties actually can be set and filter everything else out. Less fancy is perhaps a list of properties to exclude, but even then it\u0027d either require inventing a wrapper abstraction for all property assignments, or a bunch of ad-hoc if tests which aren\u0027t much better than my skipStl hack in PS6.\n\n3. Function pointer or factory abstraction to allow member types to specialise PopulateFromVariant.\n\n#3 seems viable, at the cost of the usual Go boilerplate required for such a thing, but I\u0027m open for suggestions.",
      "parentUuid": "5b39a871_f2b76ded",
      "revId": "2598b7091beb47a50a184037d3a31b412d8ddc16",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
